event.content:
The core topics at the course in 2018 will be:
- operational use of satellite data for non-frontal meso-scale weather systems; 
- use of different satellite products derived from the SAF network and the latest multispectral environmental satellite applications;
- communication between different national meteorological services, the media and the general public from a forecaster's perspective.

event.learningOutcome:
Participants will be able to: 
- Understand what are the factors that determine additional weather phenomena  hail, wind gusts, heavy precipitation etc. - in non-frontal features and how they can be forecast using satellite images; 
- Interpret different non-frontal features using satellite images; 
- Improve communication skills between forecasters and clients, media and public, especially on local weather features; 
- Apply gained knowledge to improve forecasting skills in local weather features and phenomena.

Participants will learn to:
Describe tropical cyclone climatology in the Indian Ocean (where and when they form);
Identify distinguishing features of tropical cyclone structure;
Describe ingredients needed for tropical cyclone genesis;
Define the stages of a tropical cyclone lifecycle;
Detect cloud types in tropical cyclones using satellite remote sensing;
Detect TC centre by use of IR/VIS imagery, microwave imagery/products and scatterometer winds;
Learn key microwave techniques for estimating rainfall in the next 24/48 hours;
Detect changes in tropical cyclone intensity using satellite remote sensing, in particular the use of the Dvorak TC intensity estimation technique;
Describe key storm and environmental factors that affect  tropical cyclone intensity;
Describe the mechanisms that influence tropical cyclone motion;
Describe the basic mechanisms for tropical cyclone hazards particularly those at landfall.

